<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leiden</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Leyden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leiden is a city in South Holland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, on the Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. It withstood a Spanish siege in 1574, relieved when the dykes were cut, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> rewarded it with the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> university in 1575. A great cloth-making city in the 17th century, it also sheltered English Separatists who later sailed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayflower">Mayflower</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rembrandt">Rembrandt</a> was born there in 1606.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leiden.jpg" alt="Leiden" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roger Veringmeier · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rapenburg_Leiden_Centrum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Law was plural: European codes applied to Europeans, while for Indonesians customary law (adat), studied by Cornelis van Vollenhoven and his school at <b>Leiden</b>, was recognised alongside Islamic law in family matters.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Immigrants from the southern Netherlands, especially after the fall of Antwerp in 1585, brought capital and skills to <b>Leiden</b>’s textile industry and Amsterdam’s trade, and prosperous burghers sustained a large open market for paintings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>English scholars also glossed Latin texts, first in the margins or between the lines, then in word-lists such as the Épinal-Erfurt, <b>Leiden</b> and Corpus Glossaries, which grew into Latin–Old English glossaries with some of the character of dictionaries.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhsin Mahdi's <b>Leiden</b> edition (1984), built on the Galland manuscript, is the only critical edition to date; none has been made of the larger ZER text.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic had the highest literacy rates in Europe, and universities became career pathways to public office; the University of <b>Leiden</b>, founded in 1575, drew scholars from all over Europe.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts drained peat bogs, built dykes and founded towns such as Haarlem, Delft, <b>Leiden</b> and Dordrecht.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holland">County of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
